From 264423170398c114aac1dc2f358c7406ee90545c Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Adam DePrince <adam.deprince@gmail.com>
Date: Fri, 20 Nov 2026 17:34:28 -0500
Subject: [RFC PATCH 1/5] proc: add /proc/dnuma_view, a description of CPUs and
 work on other machines

A cluster manager (dnuma-master) moves the execution of ordinary processes
to other machines while the processes themselves stay here: they keep
their pid, their files and their place in the process tree, and sleep
while their code runs elsewhere. Tools then show such a process as idle
and know nothing of the CPUs it is using.

Add a control file through which the manager can describe those CPUs
(their /proc/cpuinfo text and their /proc/stat counters) and, per task,
the remote CPU it is on, whether it is running there and the CPU time it
has consumed there. This patch only stores what is written; the following
patches make /proc/cpuinfo, /proc/stat, /proc/<pid>/stat and
/proc/<pid>/status show it.

The file is root-only and has one writer at a time. Everything the writer
described is dropped when it closes the file, so a manager that dies takes
its view with it, and entries of tasks that have exited are dropped on the
next write. Readers will run under RCU and behind a static key that is
enabled only while a writer has the file open. Remote CPUs must be
numbered from nr_cpu_ids up, so they can never be confused with a CPU this
machine has or may get.

The interface is described at the top of fs/proc/dnuma_view.c.

New config option: CONFIG_DNUMA_VIEW (x86-64), default off.

Assisted-by: Claude Fable 5.2
Co-Authored-By: Claude Fable 5.2 <noreply@anthropic.com>
Claude-Session: https://claude.ai/code/session_018DNkjq1BzRe4hiXNhh3ctv
